# Rule: SwiftData Persistence Lifecycle

Sources

https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swiftdata/preserving-your-apps-model-data-across-launches
https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swiftdata/adding-and-editing-persistent-data-in-your-app
https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swiftdata/deleting-persistent-data-from-your-app
https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swiftdata/reverting-data-changes-using-the-undo-manager
https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swiftdata/modelcontext
https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swiftdata/modelcontainer

Scope

Applies to:

- create/update/delete lifecycle operations
- save boundary semantics and autosave behavior
- batch delete flows and destructive mutation handling
- undo manager integration with context lifecycles

Intent

Make persistence commits explicit and recoverable so UI state, model state,
and storage state stay consistent under both normal and destructive workflows.

Core Principle

Lifecycle operations are context-mediated and save-boundary aware.

Implementation Rules

- Insert new model graphs via root insertion into a context.
- Let context track mutations; call `save()` when explicit commit boundaries are required.
- Treat autosave behavior as a design choice; do not assume manual save paths if autosave is enabled.
- For destructive operations, clear UI selection state before delete when needed.
- Use batch delete API (`delete(model:where:includeSubclasses:)`) for mass operations.
- Enable undo intentionally with model container configuration when product workflow requires it.
- Remember undo automation applies to main context, not arbitrary background contexts.

Canonical Patterns

```swift
let invoice = Invoice(...)
modelContext.insert(invoice)
try modelContext.save()
```

```swift
try modelContext.delete(model: Invoice.self, where: #Predicate { $0.isArchived })
```

Anti-Patterns

- Performing delete flows without reconciling selection/detail UI state.
- Assuming undo automatically covers all contexts/threads.
- Mixing staged edit state and direct model mutation without explicit commit/cancel semantics.
- Ignoring save failures in explicit-save workflows.

Checklist

- Are insert/update/delete operations context-based and intentional?
- Is save strategy explicit (autosave vs manual)?
- Are delete paths safe for UI selection and relationships?
- Is undo enabled only where product behavior requires it?

Architectural Implications

- Lifecycle operations define transactional semantics between UI, domain state, and persistence.
- Save strategy (autosave/manual) must be explicit per workflow to avoid ambiguous commit boundaries.
- Delete and undo behavior must preserve both data integrity and UI consistency.

Advanced Notes

- Context tracks changes automatically; update flow concerns boundary timing and failure handling.
- Undo automation is main-context scoped; background contexts need explicit strategy.
- Batch delete APIs are preferred for large-scale removal operations.

Integration With Architecture

- Keep create/edit/delete orchestration in feature-level use-case layers where possible.
- Ensure list/detail selection models are reconciled before destructive operations.
- Align undo availability with product UX expectations in editing-heavy flows.

Enforcement Summary

Always prefer:

- explicit context-mediated lifecycle operations
- clear commit/cancel boundaries
- safe delete behavior with selection reconciliation
- deliberate undo enablement

Never allow:

- implicit commit semantics in high-risk workflows
- delete flows that leave stale UI selection pointers
- silent save failure handling
- assuming undo applies across all contexts automatically
